We report a dopant-free polymeric hole transport material (HTM) that is based on benzo[1,2-b:4,5:b’]dithiophene and 2,1,3-benzothiadiazole and results in highly efficient and stable perovskite solar cells $(\sim17.3\%$ over $^{1400\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $75\%$ humidity). The HTM comprises a random copolymer (RCP), which is characterized using UV–vis absorption spectroscopy, cyclic voltammetry, space-charge-limited current, and grazing-incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering. The RCP-based perovskite solar cell exhibits the highest efficiency $(17.3\%)$ in the absence of dopants [lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide and tert-butylpyridine]. The observed efficiency is attributed to a deep HOMO energy level and high hole mobility. In addition, the long-term stability of the device is dramatically improved by avoiding deliquescent or hygroscopic dopants and by introducing the hydrophobic polymer layer. RCP devices maintain their initial efficiency over 1400 h at $75\%$ humidity, whereas devices made from HTMs with additives fail after 900 h.
## Broader context
Perovskite hybrid solar cells have recently been the subject of extensive investigations. The hole transport layer performs a particularly important role in transporting holes and preventing direct contact with moisture in the air. However, hole transport materials (HTMs) require deliquescent and hygroscopic dopants, which are detrimental to device lifetimes due to their low hole mobilities. In this study, we develop RCP, a dopant-free polymeric HTM. RCP exhibits high hole mobility $(3.09\times10^{-3}\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ without any dopants, promoting efficient charge transport to the counter electrodes. Empirically, the highest efficiency achieved was $17.3\%$ in a planar perovskite solar cell; this result was highly reproducible. The hydrophobic RCP layer coated on the perovskite layer functioned as a moisture barrier, preventing direct contact between the perovskite and moisture in the air. The long-term stability of devices was dramatically improved at various humidity levels. At a humidity of $75\%$ , the device maintained its initial efficiency for $1400\mathrm{~h~}$ . Our newly developed polymeric HTM guarantees both efficiency and stability in perovskite solar cells, boosting research in this area.
## Introduction
Organometal halide perovskites $\mathsf{I A B X}_{3}$ , A $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ organic cation, $\textsf{B}=$ metal cation, and $\mathsf{X}=$ halide) are promising materials for highly efficient solar cells.1-5 They have outstanding light-harvesting properties, a high absorption coefficient, and ambipolar characteristics including high charge mobility (approximately $10^{1}c m^{2}\cup^{-1}s^{-1})$ , and a long carrier diffusion length (approximately $100{-}1000\ \mathsf{n m}_{\cdot}$ ).6-9 Various types of structures have been investigated, including mesoscopic,10, 11 mesosuperstructured,12 planar,13-15 and inverted planar.16-18 In general, a bilayer of the perovskite and hole transport layer (HTL) is sandwiched between a working electrode, such as fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO), and a counter electrode, such as gold (Au) or silver (Ag). Numerous studies have examined the device physics of perovskite solar cells and reported high power conversion efficiencies.19-25 Perovskites with various compositions have been developed by fabrication methods such as sequential deposition,26 one-step spin coating,27 solvent engineering,28 vapor deposition,29 and vapor-assisted solution processing.13 The highest certified efficiency over $20\%$ was recently reported for a solar cell based on formamidinium lead iodide, which was fabricated by an intramolecular exchange process.30 Devices with compact layers such as zinc oxide $\left(\ Z\mathsf{n O}\right),^{31}$ tin oxide $(\mathsf{S n O}_{2})$ ,32, 33 tungsten oxide $(\mathsf{W O}_{\times}),^{34}$ and quantum dots35 have been reported. Among them, a $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ compact layer exhibited high reproducibility and an efficiency $(17.21\%$ ) comparable to that of a conventional titanium oxide $\left(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\right)$ layer. In most reports, triarylamine-based materials such as $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis $(\mathsf{N},\mathsf{N}^{\prime}$ -di- $\cdot\mathsf{p}$ -methoxyphenylamine)- $^{.9,9^{\prime}}$ - spirobifluorene (spiro-MeOTAD) and polytriarylamine (PTAA) were incorporated into devices along with dopants. The fabrication of a uniform and pinhole-free perovskite layer is an essential prerequisite for highly efficient solar cells.36-38 Hole transport materials (HTMs) must be further developed to achieve high power conversion efficiency and stability in perovskite solar cells. The HOMO energy level of the HTM must be higher than that of the perovskite at the interface in order to achieve effective hole transfer from perovskite to HTL (case 1 in Fig. 1a). Hole mobility in the HTL must be reasonably high; however, most HTMs including spiro-MeOTAD and PTAA exhibit low hole mobility $(\sim10^{-5}\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ in their pristine forms (case 2 in Fig. 1b) even though the HOMO energy level of the HTM is well optimized. This results in slower hole transport in the HTL and decreased efficiency. Hence, most HTMs are doped with additives such as lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI) and tertbutylpyridine (tBP), resulting in increased hole mobility (case 3 in Fig. 1a).39 This strategy has been largely adopted for highly efficient solar cells based on spiro-MeOTAD, PTAA, and similarly new HTMs.40-47 The dopants used with these HTMs are deliquescent and hygroscopic; they degrade the perovskite film as well as the organic HTL, diminishing the stability of the device.48 HTMs with high hole mobility must be developed to overcome this challenge. The next generation of HTMs should have not only a properly tuned HOMO energy level but also high hole mobility (e.g., $\sim10^{-3}\ c m^{2}\ \vee^{-1}\ s^{-1})$ in the absence of dopants (so-called dopant-free HTMs). Despite substantial research efforts, few dopant-free HTMs have been reported: a tetrathiafulvalene derivative,49 an acene derivative,50 spirotype small molecules,51 and donor–acceptor (D–A) conjugated small molecules.52, 53 The best efficiency $(16.1\%)$ was reported for the additive-free, D–A conjugated small molecules.53 Quite recently, Trux-OMeTAD (composed of a truxene-core with arylamine terminals) exhibited $18.6\%$ efficiency in an inverted planar structure. Dopant-free polymeric HTMs have been studied less frequently; they exhibit efficiencies around $12\%$ or less.54, 55
Device efficiency could be significantly improved using a thinner HTL, which resulted in a shorter pathway.56 For example, a PTAA layer around $100\ \mathsf{n m}$ thick completely covered a rough perovskite layer,30, 36 whereas layer thickness of over $200\ \mathsf{n m}$ is generally required for spiro-MeOTAD or small-molecule HTMs to obtain highly efficient perovskite solar cells.57, 58 This suggests that an ideal HTL can be realized using a thin layer of polymeric HTM with a proper HOMO energy level and high hole mobility (case 4 in Fig. 1a).
Previously, we reported the development of new polymers including D–A conducting polymers for optoelectronics applications. Our work rigorously studied energy transfer phenomena, energy conversion efficiency, and long-term stability in dye-sensitized solar cells and organic solar cells.60-67 The advantages of D-A type conducting polymers are in their superior and tunable optical and electrical properties such as energy levels, low bandgap, high mobility and good film formability.68 We recently reported that a D–A conducting polymer that contained a diketopyrrolopyrrole moiety exhibited better stability than spiro-MeOTAD in perovskite solar cells due to the hydrophobicity of the polymer.69 We also demonstrated how the physical and electronic properties of D–A conducting polymers could be tuned for their application to perovskite solar cells.70
Herein, we report a novel D–A polymeric HTM based on benzo[1,2-b:4,5:b’]dithiophene (BDT) and 2,1,3- benzothiadiazole (BT) (Fig. 1b). BDT is the most commonly used moiety due to its high coplanarity, which enhances $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, thereby facilitating charge transport.71, 72 O n the other hand, a BT moiety, one of the strongest electron acceptors, can lower the HOMO energy level in D–A conducting polymers.71 A good candidate for a dopant-free HTM should exhibit high hole mobility and a slightly higher
HOMO energy level than the perovskite (Fig. 1a). We first focused on P–OR; its reported hole mobility was high $(\sim10^{-3}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ .73 However, P–OR had a deep HOMO energy level $(-5.51\ \mathrm{eV})$ compared to that of $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ (–5.43 eV). To raise the HOMO energy level up to $-5.43\ \mathrm{eV}$ , we combined P–OR with $P-R$ , which had nearly same structure and a higher HOMO energy level.74 The random copolymer (RCP) formed by combining these polymers was expected to have high hole mobility and a favorable HOMO energy level (Fig. 1b). A device in the configuration of $\mathsf{F T O/S n O_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b l_{3}/R C P/A u}$ produced the highest efficiency in this study $(17.3\%)$ without any dopants; it demonstrated outstanding stability, functioning for more than $1400\ \mathsf{h}$ at $75\%$ humidity. These metrics are far superior to those of spiro-MeOTAD devices ( $15.3\%$ ; $0\%$ after $900\mathsf{h}$ ).
## Results and discussion
The general synthesis and purification procedures for P–OR, P– R, and RCP are fully described in the experimental section. Each polymer was characterized using nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy, gel permeation chromatography (GPC), differential scanning calorimetry (DSC), and atomic force microscopy (AFM). All polymers had similar molecular weights and exhibited mostly amorphous characteristics [see the Electronic Supporting Information for NMR (Fig. S1), GPC (Fig. S2), DSC (Fig. S3), and AFM (Fig. S4) data]. The properties of the polymers were characterized by UV–vis absorption, cyclic voltammetry (CV), space-chargelimited current (SCLC), and grazing-incidence wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS).
