The normalized UV–vis absorption spectra are shown in Fig. 2a for films of P–OR, $P-R,$ , and RCP. (See Fig. S5 for the UV–vis absorption spectra of P–OR, $P-R.$ , and RCP in chlorobenzene.) The corresponding data $(\lambda_{\mathrm{{max}}},\ \lambda_{\mathrm{{edge}}},$ optical bandgap, and energy levels) are listed in Table 1. CV was used to analyze the electrochemical properties of the polymers. The oxidation potentials of each polymer is shown in Fig. 2b, and the HOMO energy levels are calculated to be –5.51, $-5.40$ , and $-5.41\ \mathrm{eV}$ for P–OR, P–R, and RCP, respectively. As expected, the energy level of RCP was slightly higher than that of $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ $(-5.43$ eV) because the characteristics of P–OR and P–R were properly combined in RCP.
Mobility measurements of pristine P–OR, P–R, and RCP were performed using the SCLC method with the following diode structure: ITO/PEDOT:PSS/polymer/Au for hole-only devices, and the results were fitted to an SCLC model. The charge carrier mobilities were calculated using the SCLC model (Fig. 2c);75 the mobilities of P–R and P–OR were $0.65\times{{10}^{-3}}$ and $1.85\times10^{-3}\ {\mathsf{c m}}^{2}\ {\mathsf{V}}^{-1}{\mathsf{s}}^{-1}$ , respectively, similar to the reported values for analogous polymers in which the alkyl substituents were slightly different.73 The hole mobility of RCP was $3.09\times$ ${{10}^{-3}}\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}{\mathsf{s}}^{-1}$ , which was even greater than that of P–OR. The addition of LiTFSI $(2~\mathsf{m M})$ and t-BP $(0.07~\mathsf{m M}_{\cdot}^{\prime}$ ) to the polymers increased their hole mobilities to $0.78\times10^{-3}$ (P–R), $2.38~\times\$ ${{10}^{-3}}$ (P–OR), and $3.34\times10^{-3}\mathsf{c m}^{2}\mathsf{V}^{-1}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ (RCP) (Fig. S6). These increases were not larger because the polymers inherently exhibited high hole mobilities, in contrast with triarylaminebased HTMs. Along with the increase in hole mobility, effective carrier density in each polymer was also increased when dopants were incorporated (Fig. S7). To investigate the origin of the high hole mobility observed in RCP, we performed GIWAXS measurements on the polymers (Figs. 2d and 2e). The X-ray diffraction (XRD) profiles (Fig. 2d) of all polymers showed a $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking peak (010) along the out-of-plane direction and a lamellar peak (100) along the in-plane direction. This indicated that all the polymers have the face-on orientation on the UV–ozone-treated, polar, silicon substrate. The RCP film exhibited stronger peaks than the P–OR and P–R in both directions due to a more strongly directed face-on orientation, as shown in Fig. 2e. These results explained why the hole mobility of RCP was even greater than that of P–R and P–OR. We tuned the HOMO energy levels of the polymers to that of the perovskite (Fig. 3a). The engineered HOMO energy level and high hole mobility of RCP were designed to work as a dopant-free HTM.
We fabricated perovskite solar cells in various configurations (Table S2); the optimum was a planar device in the configuration $\mathsf{F T O/S n O_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b l_{3}/H T M s/A u}$ (Figs. 3b, S8 and 3c). The current density–voltage (J–V) characteristics were measured under simulated air mass 1.5 global (AM 1.5G) solar irradiation for devices made with each HTM. Devices with P–OR and P–R exhibited low efficiencies irrespective of the presence of dopants (ADDs: 2mM LiTFSI and $0.07~\mathsf{m M}~{\sf t B P}$ ) as shown in Fig. 4a. These low efficiencies were ascribed to too deep HOMO energy levels or low hole mobility. In contrast, the perovskite solar cell with RCP exhibited high efficiency with and without ADDs. The device without dopants achieved $17.3\%$ efficiency with a $\ensuremath{1.08\vee}$ open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{{oc}}})_{\cdot}$ , a $21.9\:\mathsf{m A}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ short circuit current $(\boldsymbol{J}_{\mathsf{S C}}).$ , and a $75.0\%$ fill factor (FF); the performance was similar in the presence of ADDs. The measured efficiency $(17.3\%)$ is the highest value reported for a conventional planar device. In the case of spiro-MeOTAD devices, ADDs were necessary to reach an efficiency comparable to that of the RCP device (Fig. 4b). The fabricated device showed hysteresis, which has been observed in other planar perovskite solar cells (Fig. 4c and Table S3).28, 32, 76 The $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ value was confirmed by measuring the incident photon-to-current efficiency (IPCE), as shown in Fig. 4d. The RCP device exhibited a statistically higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and FF, but lower $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ than the spiro-MeOTAD device with ADDs (Figure S9).
The higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ was attributed to the deep HOMO energy level of RCP. The higher FF was one of the advantages when using a polymer as a HTM: small molecules spin coated onto the perovskite layer formed a flat layer that did not make contact along the rough perovskite surface, whereas polymers intimately contacted the rough perovskite surface (Fig. S10). Consequently, polymers increased charge collection related to the FF by forming a thin layer along the perovskite layer. The difference in $J_{\mathsf{S C}}$ values was attributed to the slightly higher hole mobility of spiro-MeOTAD with ADDs than that of RCP.57
To investigate the ability of RCP to extract holes, we measured steady-state and transient photoluminescence (PL) decay. We prepared 3 samples: glass $\mathsf{\prime C H_{3}N H_{3}P b l_{3}},$ , $\mathsf{\Pi}_{3}|{\mathsf{a s s}}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}/{\mathsf{R C P}}.$ , and glass/ $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}/$ spiro-MeOTAD with ADDs. The perovskite exhibited strong PL peak near $760~\mathsf{n m}$ , as shown in Fig. 4e, and PL was largely quenched when RCP or spiroMeOTAD with ADDs was coated onto the perovskite, meaning that the holes were largely extracted into the HTM. From the transient PL decays (Fig. 4f), the lifetimes of holes were calculated: 30.3 ns for glas $\mathsf{s}/{\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3},$ 4.7 ns for $\mathsf{g l a s s/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b l_{3}/R C P}$ , and 5.3 ns for $\mathsf{g l a s s/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b l_{3}/s p i r}$ oMeOTAD with ADDs. RCP reduced the lifetime of holes more significantly than spiro-MeOTAD, which meant that RCP extracted holes more efficiently. The results from steady-state and transient PL decays confirmed that RCP was a good HTM; it exhibited the highest efficiency in perovskite solar cells of all the HTMs tested.
LiTFSI and tBP have generally been used to increase the conductivity and mobility of HTMs.39 However, LiTFSI absorbs moisture in the air, which degrades the perovskite.48, 77 LiTFSI saturates from ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ in the air after just $2.5\ h$ at humidities of $60\%$ and $35\%$ (Fig. S11). When a small quantity of LiTFSI was scattered on the perovskite film, the black perovskite turned into yellow lead iodide as LiTFSI absorbed the moisture in the air (Fig. S11). tBP also affected the perovskite. The absorption spectra of the perovskite decreased as higher concentrations (0, 2, and $10\ \mathrm{vol\%}$ ) of tBP were added to the perovskite film; finally, tBP completely washed off the perovskite (Fig. S12). These phenomena were observed by AFM and scanning electron microscopy (Figs. S13 and S14). The rms roughness value of the film decreased rapidly as the ratio of tBP increased, indicating that tBP washed off the rough perovskite surface. It is likely that tBP degraded the perovskite by forming a $[\mathsf{P b}|_{2}\cdot\mathtt{t B P}]$ ] coordinated complex.78 Alternately, iodine may have reacted with the tBP to form an iodopyridinate complex.79 Though a small amount of dopants is used in perovskite solar cells, they can have a bad influence on the perovskite, degrade perovskite, and finally shorten the device lifetime. From these phenomena, high stability of device is expected to be obtained by avoiding deliquescent and hygroscopic ADDs.
Fig. 5 (a) Stability test at $25\%$ humidity, (b) stability test at $75\%$ humidity, (c) the time-lapsed efficiency curves for different HTMs, and (d) timelapsed images of perovskite solar cells with RCP, RCP with ADDs, and spiro-MeOTAD with ADDs.
Finally, the long-term stability of perovskite solar cells without encapsulation was tested at low and high humidities: $25\%$ and $75\%$ . At $25\%$ humidity, the efficiency of the RCP device was nearly constant over $1400\ \mathsf{h}.$ , whereas the spiro-MeOTAD device with ADDs exhibited a slightly decreased efficiency (Figs. 5a and S15). The RCP device exhibited greater stability due to its hydrophobicity (Fig. S16). At $75\%$ humidity, the trends of different HTMs were more distinct (Figs. 5b, 5c, and S17). The efficiency of the spiro-MeOTAD device decreased rapidly after $400\ h$ and vanished after $900\ \mathsf{h}$ . The weak resistance of this device to moisture was attributed to the dopants and the low hydrophobicity of spiro-MeOTAD. The RCP device exhibited much greater stability, although the efficiency decreased rapidly after $900\mathrm{~h~}$ when ADDs were used. The RCP device was remarkably stable and maintained its initial efficiency for 1400 h; this was attributed to its lack of deliquescent or hygroscopic ADDs and its hydrophobicity. These results demonstrated the deleterious effects of ADDs on the perovskite. Images of the perovskite solar cells with RCP, RCP with ADDs, and spiroMeOTAD with ADDs are shown in Fig. 5d. At $75\%$ humidity, the moisture in the air slowly penetrated into the perovskite layer and decomposed it into yellow $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ . When ADDs were incorporated into the HTM, yellow $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ appeared more quickly; the perovskite was decomposed completely in the spiro-MeOTAD device with ADDs after $900\ \mathsf{h}$ . These results demonstrate that ADDs significantly decrease the stability of perovskite solar cells and that polymer acts as a more effective moisture barrier than a small molecule.
## Conclusions
In summary, we report a dopant-free polymeric HTM, RCP, that is used to produce highly efficient perovskite solar cells with long-term stability. RCP is based on BDT and BT; it exhibits a deep HOMO energy level $(-5.41~\mathrm{eV})$ and high hole mobility $(\sim10^{-3}\ c m^{2}\lor^{-1}s^{-1})$ in the absence of dopants (LiTFSI and tBP). This results from the combination of P–OR, which has high hole mobility, with $P-R,$ , which has a reasonably deep
HOMO energy level. These superior electronic properties make it possible to fabricate dopant-free devices with high efficiencies. The measured efficiency $(17.3\%)$ is the highest reported for dopant-free HTMs in conventional planar devices. In addition, the long-term stability of devices is significantly improved by introducing hydrophobic RCP and avoiding deliquescent and hygroscopic dopants. RCP devices maintained their initial efficiency for more than $^{1400\mathrm{~h~}}$ at humidities of $25\%$ and $75\%$ . These results suggest a broad strategy for developing efficient HTMs. This demonstration of the detrimental effects of additives on devices is expected to accelerate the study of dopant-free HTMs.
